---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/expert/behavioural-finance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Behavioural finance asks <strong>what happens to markets when the traders are human</strong>.
    It keeps both halves of the truth: markets are remarkably efficient at absorbing information
    (Fama, Nobel 2013) <em>and</em> demonstrably prone to bubbles, panics and persistent
    anomalies (Shiller, Nobel 2013 — the shared prize is the field's own verdict). The unit's
    three topics provide the structure: how humans actually measure return and risk, how they
    actually build portfolios, and what their behaviour does to pricing models. The synthesis:
    efficiency is the long-run gravity; psychology is the weather — and the weather is tradeable
    enough to matter.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="efficiency, anomalies, mechanisms, and why arbitrage can't fix everything" widget="equationStep" config={behavfinCfg} />

  <h2>Return and risk, as humans measure them</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Prospect theory.</strong> Kahneman and Tversky (1979) replaced the rational value
    function with a measured one: outcomes are evaluated against a <em>reference point</em>
    (usually the purchase price, not wealth), losses loom about twice as large as equivalent
    gains, and the curve flattens near the reference point (risk-seeking for losses, risk-
    averse for gains). Explained facts the standard model could not: why investors hold
    underwater stocks (selling crystallises a loss), why insurers sell deductibles, why
    gamblers chase.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Loss aversion, with numbers.</strong> The measured value function prices a loss at
    roughly twice a gain: winning 100 feels like +1 unit of value; losing 100 feels like about
    −2.2. The arithmetic explains portfolio behaviour the rational model calls error: a
    50/50 coin flip offering plus-or-minus 100 is refused (subjective expected value ≈ −0.6),
    while the same coin flipped over an existing <em>loss</em> is eagerly accepted — traders
    doubling down are consistent prospect-theory agents, not stupid ones. The equity-premium
    application follows: with frequent evaluations, the market's volatility delivers regular
    loss experiences at double weight, and only a premium of historical size makes the
    subjective ledger positive — the premium as the price of being watched.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The puzzle it explains.</strong> Stocks have historically outperformed Treasury
    bills by roughly 5–6% a year (geometric) — more than any plausible risk aversion justifies
    (Mehra and Prescott's 1985 equity premium puzzle). Benartzi and Thaler's behavioural answer:
    <em>myopic loss aversion</em>. Investors check their portfolios frequently; each check offers
    a chance to feel a loss at double weight; the shorter the evaluation horizon, the bigger the
    premium needed to hold stocks at all. Calibrating the model to plausible check frequencies
    reproduces the observed premium — the rare behavioural result that solves a puzzle with
    numbers, not narrative.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mental accounting.</strong> People file money in non-fungible buckets (dividends
    feel like spending money; a house's paper gain feels different from salary), which distorts
    measured risk-taking: the same dollar is treated differently depending on which mental
    ledger it sits in. The measurement layer of finance — risk tolerances, questionnaires,
    suitability rules — is calibrated against these distortions, which is why good suitability
    practice asks about <em>behaviour in past drawdowns</em>, not stated preferences.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Framing, measured.</strong> The reference point does the damage: identical outcomes
    behave differently depending on their description. Tversky and Kahneman's disease experiment
    (<em>Science</em>, 1981) offered two logically identical programmes: framed as "200 people
    will be saved", 72% of subjects took the certain option; the same programmes framed as
    "400 people will die" took the gamble for 78% of subjects. The invariance axiom of rational
    choice — descriptions should not matter — fails measurably, and the failure scales straight
    into finance: "locking in a gain" and "missing further upside" are the same trade under
    different frames, which is why a screen showing unrealised losses (frame: loss) produces
    different behaviour than the identical numbers shown as returns since purchase (frame:
    gain). Prospect theory without framing is half the theory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "loss aversion means people always avoid risky losses." The
    reference-point structure predicts the opposite in one region: once in the loss domain,
    people become risk-<em>seeking</em> — doubling down to reach break-even. That is why losing
    traders increase position sizes, and why "get back to even" is the most expensive sentence
    in markets.
  </p>

  <h2>Portfolio construction, as humans actually do it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Naive diversification.</strong> Benartzi and Thaler (2001) found retirement savers
    allocate roughly evenly across whatever menu of funds they are offered — 1/n, whatever n
    is. Widening the choice set from one stock fund and one bond fund to fifty funds shifted
    equity allocations dramatically: menu design is asset allocation. The policy invention that
    follows — auto-enrolment and default funds (Save More Tomorrow, Thaler and Benartzi, 2004:
    commit future raises to saving) — raised participation rates by tens of percentage points
    in the US and UK with no coercion at all. Choice architecture beat exhortation; the Nobel
    committee cited it (2017).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The disposition effect.</strong> Shefrin and Statman (1985) named the pattern
    Odean (1998) then measured across 10,000 retail accounts: investors realise gains about
    1.5 times as readily as losses, selling winners to bank the feeling and holding losers to
    avoid the regret. The tax arithmetic runs the other way (gains are taxable, losses
    deductible) — so the behaviour is predictably value-destroying. Barber and Odean's companion
    finding: overconfident men traded 45% more than women and earned roughly a percentage point
    a year less; the most active households underperformed the market by about 6.5 points
    annually. Trading is expensive precisely because it feels skilled.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Home bias.</strong> Investors everywhere overweight domestic equities — US
    investors hold roughly double the home-country share that global market weights imply —
    sacrificing diversification for familiarity. The candidate mechanisms (familiarity,
    information asymmetry, implicit hedging of local liabilities) each explain part; none makes
    the portfolio efficient. It is the cleanest demonstration that portfolio construction in
    practice is identity plus inertia, with optimisation a distant third.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "cash is the risk-free asset." In nominal terms, yes; in what
    money buys, no — inflation grinds purchasing power continuously, and the inflation-indexed
    comparison, the only one that matters to a saver, makes idle cash a steadily losing position
    in most decades. The same mental accounting that files cash as "safe" files nominal house
    prices as "wealth", which is how households end up maximising the feeling of safety while
    minimising its substance.
  </p>

  <h2>What human traders do to pricing: CAPM and its anomalies</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The beta anomaly, behaviourally.</strong> CAPM says high-beta assets earn more.
    The data say the line is flat: low-beta portfolios match or beat high-beta ones after
    risk-adjustment. The behavioural explanation is a taste story — investors <em>prefer</em>
    lottery-like, high-beta glamour stocks (paying a premium for upside skew, which depresses
    those assets' expected returns), reinforced by leverage constraints that push risk-seekers
    into high-beta stocks instead of borrowing (Frazzini and Pedersen's betting-against-beta
    mechanism). A pricing anomaly became a preference measurement.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Momentum, the anomaly that survived.</strong> Jegadeesh and Titman (1993) showed
    that stocks winning over the past year keep beating losers by roughly 12% a year (about 1%
    a month) over the following months — a pattern no risk-based model wants, since buying
    recent winners looks like risk-adjusted free money, and the effect has since been
    replicated across international markets. Behavioural readings (initial underreaction to
    news, then delayed overreaction — Barberis, Shleifer and Vishny's 1998 model) fit the
    signature; the anomaly's persistence after publication, punctuated by violent crashes (the
    momentum crash of 2009), makes it the field's central test case for whether anomalies are
    risk in disguise or psychology left on the table.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Disagreement plus constraints.</strong> Miller (1977) supplied the machinery: when
    investors disagree and pessimists cannot short (or face high costs), prices reflect
    optimists — overpricing with predictable crashes when short-sale constraints relax. The
    dot-com peak (NASDAQ −78% from March 2000 to October 2002) and GameStop (January 2021:
    from around 20 dollars to an intraday 483) are the modern case studies: disagreement, constraints,
    and social-media coordination doing what rational pricing models cannot accommodate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Limits of arbitrage.</strong> Why doesn't smart money flatten every bubble? Because
    correcting one requires capital, timing and nerve: Shleifer and Vishny (1997) formalised the
    constraint — arbitrageurs are other people's money managers, and clients withdraw exactly
    when mispricing is deepest. "Markets can stay irrational longer than you can stay solvent"
    is not a quip but the model: noise-trader risk, funding risk and career risk cap the
    correction, which is why anomalies persist and why bubbles are unshortable in practice.
    The efficient-market defence (Fama) stands alongside: ex ante, bubbles are nearly
    indistinguishable from justified repricing — "irrational exuberance" was called in 1996,
    three years before the top.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Herding formalised: information cascades.</strong> The mechanism is rational
    individually and fragile collectively: after a few early movers, each subsequent investor
    rationally reads the crowd as information and follows — private signals stop being used,
    the cascade runs, and a consensus forms that one contrary revelation can shatter
    (Bikhchandani, Hirshleifer and Welch, 1992). Reflexivity (Soros) adds the feedback loop:
    rising prices improve the fundamentals they were priced on — collateral values, confidence,
    capital access — so the boom manufactures its own justification until the mirror reverses.
    2008 was the full anatomy: a narrative ("national house prices never fall"), rating models
    (the Gaussian copula) that assumed independence where there was correlation, and a cascade
    of AAA purchases — herding, constraints and model risk compounding, then repricing at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "efficient markets means prices are <em>right</em>." Fama's
    claim is narrower: prices are hard to beat — they incorporate information to the point
    where trading against them does not pay after costs. "Hard to beat" tolerates persistent
    mispricing no one can reliably identify ex ante; "right" would require knowing true
    values, which nobody claims to. The Efficient Markets Hypothesis is a statement about
    profit opportunities, not about truth.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "behavioural finance proves the market is dumb, so beat it
    with psychology." The anomalies mostly shrink after publication (returns fall by roughly a
    third once known — consistent with arbitrage closing them), cost real money to harvest, and
    are mostly <em>warnings</em> addressed to the reader: your own overconfidence, disposition
    and herding are the exploitable resource. The practitioner's synthesis — diversify, keep
    fees low, trade rarely, pre-commit — is behavioural finance applied defensively.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>GameStop as the field's live experiment.</strong> January 2021 assembled every
    behavioural mechanism in one fortnight: heavy short interest (over 100% of the float once
    derivatives were counted) created squeeze fuel — shorts losing forced buybacks of a rising
    stock, in a mechanical loop; social-media coordination replaced institutional herding;
    and the price ran from around 20 dollars to an intraday 483 before collapsing. The
    interpretive fight (populist correction versus manipulation; market plumbing versus
    narrative) is the field's disagreement formalised — no consensus model fully prices
    coordination without organisation yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The historical canon.</strong> Tulip mania (1637) and the South Sea Bubble (1720)
    establish that bubbles predate modern markets — and Reinhart and Rogoff's survey of eight
    centuries of default and boom data establishes the pattern's regularity: "this time is
    different" is the oldest sentence in finance. The structural ingredients modern work
    isolates: leverage, new-era narratives, easy credit and dismissal of sceptics as failing to
    understand the new paradigm — each episode reassembling them in period costume.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Formal depths.</strong> The field's serious versions are mathematical, not
    anecdotal: prospect theory solved as a portfolio model (Barberis's work), heterogeneous
    beliefs in general equilibrium (Scheinkman and Xiong's bubble model — disagreement itself
    creates an option value that inflates prices), and noise-trader risk models generating
    testable predictions. The methodological point: behavioural finance earns its place by
    predicting, not by storytelling — the anecdote is the illustration, never the evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Do social-media markets create genuinely new dynamics
    (coordination without organisation — GameStop as the pilot study)? Can regulators design
    for predictable irrationality (the whole discipline of nudge-based consumer protection)? And
    does AI trading arbitrage biases away or amplify herding when models train on the same
    data? Each is live, funded and undecided — the field's weather forecast, ongoing.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Shiller, Irrational Exuberance; Thaler (Nobel 2017); Shleifer & Vishny, JF (1997); Reinhart & Rogoff (2011).</p>
</TopicPage>
